From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 10:39:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: leave the LRO AGG_NUM to the ethernet driver

The AGG_NUM field of REG_CDM_LRO_LIMIT had three writers. The
ethernet driver writes 64 when HW GRO goes on and 0 when it goes off.
The dynamic IFC code and the wifi local fastpath each wrote 18 at the
start of a session and restored a private saved copy later, the
dynamic IFC code from an ageing timer. The value in the hardware then
depended on the order of these events, and a restore of a copy saved
while HW GRO was off wrote 0 into an enabled engine.

The ethernet driver is now the only writer, so AGG_NUM is 64 while
HW GRO is on. The vendor value of 18 is dropped and not moved,
because it has no effect at the present LRO buffer size: AGG_SIZE is
16000 bytes, which is about 11 segments. If the LRO buffers grow, 64
becomes reachable and the value needs a new look.

The agg_num command and the lro_agg_num line leave both /proc files,
so that they do not report a value that is not in the hardware.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_dynamic_ifc.c |   78 ---------------------
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_wifi_lo.c     |   89 -------------------------
 2 files changed, 2 insertions(+), 165 deletions(-)

